                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c26d3902-a0ae-3fa3-8213-9e70969d35d2",
                        "entry_id": "810560c3-2474-3895-aed6-debb6e845b26",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A female python."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "29d9f14d-9078-48d1-bef0-e44037e91be0",
                                "sense_id": "c26d3902-a0ae-3fa3-8213-9e70969d35d2",
                                "text": "On the fifteenth of January, it was discovered that the pythoness had excluded rather more than a hundred dirty-white, leathery-looking eggs."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "183cb5f5-b2d2-3319-97f4-c58b9df92ed8",
                "word": "Pythoness",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "fe67d3e2-ab85-4b4b-b6f0-126a8e66e09c",
                        "entry_id": "183cb5f5-b2d2-3319-97f4-c58b9df92ed8",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Pythonesses"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "46ded4e7-bc91-3e10-90ca-2c6ede02e473",
                        "entry_id": "183cb5f5-b2d2-3319-97f4-c58b9df92ed8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The priestess of the oracle of Apollo at Delphi."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "205d98fc-4b37-44be-918d-dd91b5ba210e",
                                "sense_id": "46ded4e7-bc91-3e10-90ca-2c6ede02e473",
                                "text": "The office of Pythoness appears not to have been desirable. Either the emanations from the cavern, or some art of the managers, threw her into real convulsions. Priests entitled prophets led her to the sacred tripod, force being often necessary for that purpose, and held her on it till her frenzy rose to whatever pitch was in their judgement most fit for the occasion."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "27ee1069-314d-4423-bd89-ad14ba8aef2d",
                                "sense_id": "46ded4e7-bc91-3e10-90ca-2c6ede02e473",
                                "text": "The unconnected words which the Pythoness screamed out in her madness were arranged into sentences by the attendant priests, who could easily place them in such an order, and fill up the breaks in such a way, as to make them express whatever was most suitable to the interests of the shrine, which was the main object."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ee50ae1a-56b4-4b8a-9cfc-e17c4b122327",
                                "sense_id": "46ded4e7-bc91-3e10-90ca-2c6ede02e473",
                                "text": "Less becomingly Origen states that when the Pythoness sat down at the mouth of the cave, \"the prophetic spirit of Apollo entered her private parts\"; ..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ca29e899-daca-45a6-bcc5-c957b9fe6a51",
                                "sense_id": "46ded4e7-bc91-3e10-90ca-2c6ede02e473",
                                "text": "But at Delphi the sun-god's spiritual bride was known in the days of Herodotus as the Pythoness, and later as Pythia."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "9a4bdb1b-fcb7-4906-bf4f-0386b18c1f0c",
                        "source_entry_id": "183cb5f5-b2d2-3319-97f4-c58b9df92ed8",
                        "source_sense_id": "46ded4e7-bc91-3e10-90ca-2c6ede02e473",
                        "source_sense_description": "priestess of the oracle of Apollo at Delphi — see also Pythia",
                        "target_lang_word": "pythie",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "pythie",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "46ba3e86-3200-3490-a66b-19702ca936cb",
                                        "entry_id": "0cf8574d-8af6-3166-a98e-5190ac7200a5",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Prophétesse ; devineresse."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "500f8157-1959-4d61-b9bd-22de30bfec8f",
                                                "sense_id": "46ba3e86-3200-3490-a66b-19702ca936cb",
                                                "text": "L’esprit Frontenac l’avait envahie tout entière. Elle en était secouée comme une Pythie et quand elle fut à son étage, sa main tremblante ne pouvait introduire la clef dans la serrure."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "98ca188d-eeca-46bd-b1cd-17c3365582bd",
                                                "sense_id": "46ba3e86-3200-3490-a66b-19702ca936cb",
                                                "text": "Rien d'étonnant si le fade bonheur de mes premières années a eu parfois un goût funèbre : je devais ma liberté à un trépas opportun, mon importance à un décès très attendu. Mais quoi : toutes les pythies sont des mortes, chacun sait cela ; tous les enfants sont des miroirs de mort."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1b170bba-4146-4efb-b158-e8ebfdc5e9b8",
                                                "sense_id": "46ba3e86-3200-3490-a66b-19702ca936cb",
                                                "text": "Les yeux noirs, vêtue de blanc, très bon chic-bon genre, la pythie du siècle me reçoit dans son appartement de la banlieue ouest."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "40524e09-43ba-4ba5-87ed-2108052c3e7a",
                                                "sense_id": "46ba3e86-3200-3490-a66b-19702ca936cb",
                                                "text": "Je l’ai vu en photo, sur un canapé en velours, tapotant de la main sur l’accoudoir, avec ses cheveux invariablement blancs et son air de pythie grecque assise sur les fumerolles de l’île de Ré. Il dit qu’« avoir le sens du kairos ne préserve pas toujours de l’hubris ». Comprendre – parlant d’Emmanuel Macron – qu’on peut avoir le sens des opportunités sans avoir celui de la mesure."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "04701f5f-0576-442c-af33-28c635cc0bd6",
                        "source_entry_id": "183cb5f5-b2d2-3319-97f4-c58b9df92ed8",
